  -> rms stays at the sqrt(count) Poisson scale and does NOT drop from deg1->deg3:
     no smooth signal to interpolate (F3 not triggered).

M3 corollary  COUNT-metric adaptive search inside the sqrt(x) window (exact pi):
        n         p(n)  win=2sqrt  binary_q  interp_q
     1586        13367        230         8         9
    12613       135409        734        10         9
   100285      1303597       2282        12        13
   797355     12151679       6970        13        13
  -> INSIDE the random window interp_q ~ binary_q ~ (1/2)log2(x): interpolation gets NO traction
     (no smooth signal), so even the COUNT advantage evaporates here.  And each probe is a
     hard-zone pi-eval (~sqrt x); sieving the window once (ZERO adaptive queries) costs the same
     ~sqrt(x) and answers all -> cost-optimal p(n) is the NON-adaptive predict+sieve.  Adaptivity
     gives no COST gain and no COUNT gain in the hard zone (F4 not triggered).

CLOSURE: P10 -- adaptive pi-querying does not beat the non-adaptive analytic-predict + local-sieve recipe.
  smooth/far zone (Newton, O(log log x)) is free & non-adaptive;
  random/hard zone has width Theta(sqrt x) and no interpolable signal; reading it (one
  sieve) dominates any adaptive query strategy.  SMOOTH+RANDOM is the adaptivity boundary.
